INT. PROUTY COTTAGE - PARLOR - LATE AFTERNOON

A low-ceilinged room. A mantel crowded with china figurines. A shawl on the arm of a chair. A grandfather's walking stick leaning by the door. Rain taps the windows.

ELEANOR PROUTY, 60s, in a gray cardigan, sits in the straight-backed chair with her hands folded. GERALD, 60s, her brother-in-law, stands at the window with his back to her. CONSTABLE IDRIS, 30s, young and awkward, perches on the edge of the settee with a notebook on his knee.

IDRIS
Mrs. Prouty. I'm sorry to trouble you.

ELEANOR
It's no trouble.

IDRIS
I only have a few questions.

ELEANOR
Of course.

Gerald does not turn. His hand rests on the curtain.

IDRIS
When did you last see your husband?

ELEANOR
Tuesday morning. At breakfast.

IDRIS
And he went to the allotment?

ELEANOR
He always goes to the allotment.

IDRIS
Did he mention anyone?

ELEANOR
No.

Idris writes. The pen scratches in the silence. A clock chimes the quarter hour.

IDRIS
Was he in good spirits?

ELEANOR
(after a pause)
He was quiet.

Gerald lets the curtain fall.

GERALD
Henry was always quiet.

IDRIS
Mr. Prouty, you live next door?

GERALD
Forty years.

IDRIS
And you didn't see him Tuesday?

GERALD
I saw him walk past. Around nine.

IDRIS
Which direction?

GERALD
Toward the river.

Eleanor looks down at her hands. She turns her wedding ring a single quarter turn.

IDRIS
The allotment is the other way.

Nobody speaks. Idris closes the notebook. Gerald crosses to the mantel and straightens a china shepherdess that did not need straightening.

GERALD
Perhaps he went for a walk.

IDRIS
Perhaps.

ELEANOR
Would you like tea, Constable?

IDRIS
That would be kind.

She rises and goes to the kitchen. Gerald watches her go. When the kettle clatters in the other room, he turns to Idris with an expression that is almost relief.

GERALD
She has not slept since Tuesday.

IDRIS
No. I should think not.

Idris tucks the notebook into his pocket. He glances at the walking stick by the door. It is wet. Rain, perhaps. Or river.
